course: dictionary
lesson: 7384
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words wear many coats. The word plumb can be a verb, a noun, an adjective and an informal adverb, and each one means something different. The words around it tell you which meaning you need. «Plumb in a washing machine» is about water pipes, because the words around plumb are about a machine.
explain: A plumb line is a weight on a string. Gravity pulls the weight straight down, so the string hangs exactly upright. Builders use it to check a wall. If a wall is plumb, it stands exactly vertical. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: a plumb wall still makes sense as an upright wall, so that is the right meaning.
explain: Plumb can also be informal. «Plumb crazy» and «plumb in the middle» mean completely or exactly. «Plumb the depths» means reach the deepest, worst point, as in «plumb the depths of despair». Always look at the words around the word before you decide what it means.
fact: Plumb can be a verb, a noun, an adjective and an informal adverb.
fact: A plumb line is a weight on a string that shows true vertical, because gravity pulls it straight down.
fact: «Plumb» as an adjective means exactly vertical, perfectly upright.
fact: «Plumb the depths» means reach the deepest or worst point, as in «plumb the depths of despair».
fact: A skilled trade is a job needing special training with your hands, like plumbing; «at £60 an hour, 10 hours of jobs bring in £600 in a day before costs.»
exercise: What does plumb mean in «plumb in a washing machine»? | To ring a bell | *To fit water pipes | To paint the walls | To wash the dishes quickly
exercise: A builder hangs a plumb line beside a wall. What is the line for? | To catch fish | To tie up a tent | To measure the length of the garden | *To check that the wall is exactly upright
exercise: «At £60 an hour, 10 hours of jobs bring in £600.» How much would 10 hours bring in at £60 an hour? | £70 | £610 | *£600 | £6
test: Nadia says «plumb crazy» means a crazy pipe. What does plumb mean here? | Upright | *Completely or exactly | To fit water pipes | Very cold
test: What does «plumb the depths of despair» suggest? | Measuring a deep well for water | Going swimming in the sea | Being very tall | *Feeling as bad as possible
exam: Which of these is NOT a meaning of plumb? | Exactly vertical, perfectly upright | Completely or exactly, in informal talk | *To bake a loaf of bread | To fit water pipes
